          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            %strong Bekenstein-Hawking entropy
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entropy"} entropy
            assigned to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+hole"} black hole
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horizon"} horizon
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#interpretation_in_adscft_correspondence Interpretation in AdS/CFT correspondence
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            BH entropy is explained by a version of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT+correspondence"} AdS/CFT correspondence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+brane"} black brane
            solution in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            is the strong-coupling limit of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane"} D-brane
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+mechanism"} KK-reduction
            these black brane configurations become ordinary
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+hole"} black hole
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entropy"} entropy
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane"} D-brane
            worldvolume theories on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/event+horizon"} event horizon
            turns out to coincide with the BH entropy of the corresponding black hole.
          %p
            Detailed computations exist in particular for
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D1-brane"} D1-brane
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D5-brane"} D5-brane
            systems. This is parts of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT+correspondence"} AdS/CFT correspondence
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#AGMOO"} AGMOO, chapter 5
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+entanglement+entropy"} holographic entanglement entropy
            %li
              %p
                %em>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory+results+applied+elsewhere"} string theory results applied elsewhere
                \.
          %h3#interpretation_by_strong_coupling_limit_of_dbranes Interpretation by strong coupling limit of D-branes
          %p
            Another way to derive Bekenstein-Hawking entropy in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            is by computing the entropy of weakly coupled open strings on D-brane configurations in flat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+space"} Minkowski space
            which transmute as the coupling constant is increased to given (supersymmetric) black hole configurations. More on this is at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+holes+in+string+theory"} black holes in string theory
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
